RestTemplate vs Feign Client (and Modern Spring HTTP Clients)

Both RestTemplate and Feign call other services over HTTP.
As of Spring Framework 7 / late 2025–2026, Spring’s recommended options are different:

  1) HTTP Service Clients  → declarative style
  2) RestClient             → imperative / synchronous style
  3) WebClient              → reactive / non-blocking style

RestTemplate → deprecated (favor RestClient)
OpenFeign    → still usable, but Spring Cloud OpenFeign is feature-complete / maintenance mode


────────────────────────────────────────
Understand 3 words first (Interview Gold)

These are NOT the same axis:

1) Synchronous  = HOW execution waits
2) Reactive     = HOW I/O is handled (non-blocking)
3) Declarative  = HOW you write the code (style)

Quick meanings:

Synchronous (blocking)
  - Thread sends request and WAITS for response
  - Like calling a restaurant and staying on the phone until they confirm
  - Spring: RestClient (modern), RestTemplate (legacy)

Reactive / Non-blocking
  - Thread does NOT sit idle waiting
  - Like ordering in an app, doing other work, getting notified later
  - Spring: WebClient (Mono / Flux)

Declarative
  - You define an INTERFACE of what to call; framework does HTTP wiring
  - Like telling a travel agent “book flight to Riyadh” — you say WHAT, not HOW
  - Spring: HTTP Service Clients (@HttpExchange)
  - Older Cloud style: OpenFeign (@FeignClient)

Important:
  Declarative ≠ reactive automatically.
  A declarative client can be sync (RestClient under the hood)
  or reactive (WebClient under the hood returning Mono/Flux).


────────────────────────────────────────
Modern Spring recommendation (as of Nov 2026)

| Need                         | Use                         |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Sync / imperative HTTP       | RestClient                  |
| Reactive / non-blocking      | WebClient                   |
| Declarative interface client | HTTP Service Clients        |
| Old sync template API        | RestTemplate (deprecated)   |
| Old Spring Cloud declarative | OpenFeign (maintenance)     |

Status details:
- RestTemplate: docs deprecate it from Spring Framework 7.0; formally `@Deprecated(since="7.1", forRemoval=true)` → removal targeted for Spring Framework 8.0. Prefer RestClient.
- RestClient: modern sync fluent API (since 6.1, now the main sync choice)
- WebClient: reactive client for async/streaming
- HTTP Service Clients: `@HttpExchange` interfaces + HttpServiceProxyFactory (Spring native declarative)
- Spring Cloud OpenFeign: still works in many codebases, but feature-complete / maintenance mode → prefer HTTP Service Clients for new Spring-first projects


────────────────────────────────────────
1) RestClient — Synchronous / Imperative (modern)

RestClient restClient = RestClient.create();

String response = restClient.get()
        .uri("https://api.example.com/users/1")
        .retrieve()
        .body(String.class);

System.out.println(response);

What happens:
  - App sends HTTP request
  - Current thread waits for response
  - Then continues

Best for: traditional blocking apps / servlet MVC sync calls


────────────────────────────────────────
2) WebClient — Reactive / Non-blocking

WebClient webClient = WebClient.create();

Mono<String> response = webClient.get()
        .uri("https://api.example.com/users/1")
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(String.class);

response.subscribe(System.out::println);

What happens:
  - App sends request
  - Thread is free for other work
  - When response arrives, callback/reactive chain handles it

Mono = 0 or 1 async result
Flux = 0..N async stream

Best for: high concurrency, streaming, WebFlux apps
Note: you CAN `.block()` WebClient, but that loses the main benefit.


────────────────────────────────────────
3) HTTP Service Clients — Declarative (modern Spring)

Define interface (WHAT to call):

@HttpExchange("/users")
public interface UserClient {

    @GetExchange("/{id}")
    User getUser(@PathVariable Long id);
}

Wire proxy (usually once as a @Bean):

@Bean
UserClient userClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {
    RestClient restClient = builder
            .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
            .build();

    HttpServiceProxyFactory factory = HttpServiceProxyFactory
            .builderFor(RestClientAdapter.create(restClient))
            .build();

    return factory.createClient(UserClient.class);
}

Use:

User user = userClient.getUser(1L);

Spring generates the implementation.
You can also back the same interface style with WebClientAdapter for reactive returns.


────────────────────────────────────────
Legacy: RestTemplate (imperative sync — deprecated)

RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
User user = restTemplate.getForObject(
        "http://localhost:8081/users/1",
        User.class
);

Pros: simple, familiar, full control
Cons: verbose; deprecated in Spring 7 → migrate to RestClient

Microservice note:
Can still use `@LoadBalanced RestTemplate` + service name URL, but for new code prefer RestClient / HTTP Service Clients.


────────────────────────────────────────
Legacy / Cloud: OpenFeign (declarative — maintenance mode)

@FeignClient(name = "user-service")
public interface UserClient {

    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    User getUserById(@PathVariable("id") Long id);
}

@Autowired
private UserClient userClient;

User user = userClient.getUserById(1L);

Pros:
  - Clean interfaces
  - Service discovery + Spring Cloud LoadBalancer integration

Cons:
  - Spring Cloud dependency
  - Project is feature-complete / maintenance mode
  - For new Spring-first apps, prefer HTTP Service Clients


────────────────────────────────────────
Comparison table (current)

| Feature        | RestTemplate     | RestClient          | WebClient              | HTTP Service Clients     | OpenFeign              |
| -------------- | ---------------- | ------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------ | ---------------------- |
| Style          | Imperative       | Imperative          | Fluent reactive        | Declarative interface    | Declarative interface  |
| Blocking       | Yes              | Yes                 | No (unless .block())   | Depends on adapter       | Sync by default        |
| Spring stance  | Deprecated 7.1   | Recommended sync    | Recommended reactive   | Recommended declarative  | Maintenance mode       |
| Best for       | Legacy only      | New sync calls      | High concurrency       | Clean service APIs       | Existing Cloud apps    |


────────────────────────────────────────
When to use what (practical)

Use RestClient when:
  - New sync HTTP calls in Spring Boot
  - Replacing RestTemplate

Use WebClient when:
  - Reactive / non-blocking / streaming needed

Use HTTP Service Clients when:
  - You want Feign-like interfaces without OpenFeign
  - Clean typed API clients in pure Spring

Use OpenFeign when:
  - Existing Spring Cloud microservices already on Feign
  - Team already standardized on it (plan eventual migration)

Use RestTemplate when:
  - Maintaining old code only — do not start new features on it


